From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sunshineinabox <aqemail@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 19:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(LaunchManager): a newly focusable window is told by
 OpenGamepadUI's own record

A launched app's window gets the focus when gamescope can focus it as
OpenGamepadUI first lists it, or else when focusable_windows_updated
reports it as added (0019, 0059). The signal's "from" list does not say
that. It is the list as the core last read it, and
get_focusable_windows(), which OpenGamepadUI calls for every new window
of an app, is that read: a change OpenGamepadUI has read before the
signal is handled arrives with from equal to to, and the window is
passed over as not new. An emptied list also leaves the last one in
place (the getter returns without storing it), with the window ids the
next launch of the same game gets again (Hollow Knight's is 67108865
every time).

Such a window never got the focus. Alone, gamescope showed the game all
the same, but with no window made the focused one, so a window of
Steam's carrying the game's id could take the screen unseen by 0060.
Started beside another game, the new one stayed hidden behind it: Hollow
Knight beside a 3DS game, 5 launches in 10 (MANGMI Air X, 2026-11-27; in
the two that were traced its window was listed before gamescope could
focus it, and no signal after that had it as added).

OpenGamepadUI now keeps its own record of the running apps' windows it
has offered the focus (focus_offers.gd), and offers it to those
gamescope lists that are not in it. A window gamescope can no longer
focus is forgotten. The list holds each window's id, app id and pid:
only the ids are read. After, on the same device: Hollow Knight came up
beside the 3DS game in 8 launches of 8 (16 to 24 s), its window made the
focused one by OpenGamepadUI each time, as the emulator's was in 7
starts and Hollow Knight's in 3 launches on its own; the emulator had
the screen back 1 s after the game ended.
---
diff --git a/core/global/launch_manager.gd b/core/global/launch_manager.gd
index 600e092..1abacd7 100644
--- a/core/global/launch_manager.gd
+++ b/core/global/launch_manager.gd
@@ -73,6 +73,9 @@ var _orphan_windows := PackedInt64Array()
 var _input_manager: InputManager
 const OguiIdCache := preload("res://core/systems/launcher/ogui_id_cache.gd")
 const DeclaredProfile := preload("res://core/systems/launcher/declared_profile.gd")
+const FocusOffers := preload("res://core/systems/launcher/focus_offers.gd")
+## The running apps' windows gamescope can focus that have been offered the focus (focus_offers.gd)
+var _focus_offered := PackedInt64Array()
 ## Every running process's OGUI_ID, read from its environment only while it is new (ogui_id_cache.gd).
 var _ogui_ids := OguiIdCache.new(func(pid: int) -> Dictionary: return Reaper.get_pid_environment(pid))
 
@@ -140,7 +143,7 @@ func _init() -> void:
 			# ("Gd<T>::bind() failed, already bound"). The question then got no answer, so a window of
 			# Steam's counted as any other, and each try logged a panic (1,554 in one launch through a
 			# running Steam client, MANGMI Air X, 2026-11-27).
-			_focus_focusable_window.call_deferred(from, to, dropped)
+			_focus_focusable_window.call_deferred(to, dropped)
 		_xwayland_primary.focusable_windows_updated.connect(on_focusable_windows_changed)
 
 	# Listen for signals from the secondary Gamescope XWayland
@@ -216,10 +219,20 @@ func _on_focused_app_changed(to: int) -> void:
 	set_app_gamepad_profile(self._current_app)
 
 
-## The windows gamescope can focus changed from `from` to `to` (focusable_windows_updated, deferred):
-## gives the focus to the window that should have it now. `dropped`: the focused window had just gone.
-func _focus_focusable_window(from: PackedInt64Array, to: PackedInt64Array, dropped: bool) -> void:
+## The windows gamescope can focus changed to `to` (focusable_windows_updated, deferred): gives the
+## focus to the window that should have it now. `dropped`: the focused window had just gone.
+func _focus_focusable_window(to: PackedInt64Array, dropped: bool) -> void:
 	var baselayer_window := _xwayland_primary.baselayer_window
+	# Which of the apps' windows gamescope has newly made focusable, by our own record: the signal's
+	# "from" list missed half of them (focus_offers.gd), and a game's window then never got the
+	# focus. Alone, gamescope showed the game all the same; started beside another game it stayed
+	# hidden behind it (Hollow Knight beside a 3DS game, 5 launches in 10, MANGMI Air X, 2026-11-27).
+	var known := PackedInt64Array()
+	for app in _running:
+		known.append_array(app.window_ids)
+	var focusable := FocusOffers.windows(to)
+	var fresh := FocusOffers.fresh(focusable, known, _focus_offered)
+	_focus_offered = FocusOffers.remember(focusable, known)
 	# A window of a running app that has just become focusable gets the focus, even with
 	# another one focused: a new window is only focused once gamescope lists it
 	# (switch_to_new_window), and it may list it after the app's windows were read (a
@@ -235,8 +248,8 @@ func _focus_focusable_window(from: PackedInt64Array, to: PackedInt64Array, dropp
 			app.switch_window(app.steam_game_window(to))
 			return
 	for app in running:
-		for window in to:
-			if window in from or not window in app.window_ids:
+		for window in fresh:
+			if not window in app.window_ids:
 				continue
 			# Not one of Steam's while the game has a window: Steam shows its hidden
 			# desktop window at times during a game.
@@ -331,12 +344,16 @@ func launch(app: LibraryLaunchItem) -> RunningApp:
 	# becomes focusable later gets the focus from on_focusable_windows_changed.
 	var switch_to_new_window := func(old_windows: PackedInt64Array, new_windows: PackedInt64Array):
 		var focusable := _xwayland_primary.get_focusable_windows() if _xwayland_primary else PackedInt64Array()
+		var switched := false
 		for window in new_windows:
-			if window in old_windows:
+			if window in old_windows or not window in focusable:
 				continue
-			if window in focusable and running_app.may_focus(window, focusable):
+			# Offered the focus here: not again when gamescope's list next changes
+			if not window in _focus_offered:
+				_focus_offered.append(window)
+			if not switched and running_app.may_focus(window, focusable):
 				running_app.switch_window(window)
-				return
+				switched = true
 	running_app.window_ids_changed.connect(switch_to_new_window)
 
 	# When the focused window goes (a launcher's splash or updater window), the app's newest window
diff --git a/core/systems/launcher/focus_offers.gd b/core/systems/launcher/focus_offers.gd
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..33c9307
--- /dev/null
+++ b/core/systems/launcher/focus_offers.gd
@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+extends RefCounted
+
+## Which windows gamescope has newly made focusable, by OpenGamepadUI's own record of the ones it has
+## already offered the focus. The "from" list of focusable_windows_updated cannot tell: it is the list
+## as it was last read, by anyone, so when OpenGamepadUI reads it before the signal comes (it does
+## for every new window of an app) the signal has from equal to to; and an emptied list leaves the
+## last one in place, with the window ids the next launch of the same game gets again.
+
+
+## The window ids in gamescope's focusable list, which holds each window's id, app id and pid.
+static func windows(focusable: PackedInt64Array) -> PackedInt64Array:
+	if focusable.size() % 3 != 0:
+		return focusable
+	var ids := PackedInt64Array()
+	for i in range(0, focusable.size(), 3):
+		ids.append(focusable[i])
+	return ids
+
+
+## The windows gamescope can focus (`focusable`, ids only, its order kept) that belong to a running app
+## (`known`) and have not been offered the focus yet (`offered`).
+static func fresh(focusable: PackedInt64Array, known: PackedInt64Array, offered: PackedInt64Array) -> PackedInt64Array:
+	var out := PackedInt64Array()
+	for window in focusable:
+		if window in known and not window in offered and not window in out:
+			out.append(window)
+	return out
+
+
+## The record once `fresh` has been dealt with: the running apps' windows gamescope can focus. A window
+## it can no longer focus is forgotten, so it is offered the focus again when it comes back, and so is
+## the next window that gets its id.
+static func remember(focusable: PackedInt64Array, known: PackedInt64Array) -> PackedInt64Array:
+	var out := PackedInt64Array()
+	for window in focusable:
+		if window in known and not window in out:
+			out.append(window)
+	return out
